                <section class="content-section" id="cmdb-context">
                    <h2>CMDB Business Context</h2>
                    <p>
                        The secret weapon of SecOps is <strong>CMDB integration</strong>. When a vulnerability 
                        scanner reports a finding or a SIEM generates an alert, SecOps doesn't just show you 
                        the technical details — it shows you the <em>business impact</em>.
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Asset ownership:</strong> Which team owns the affected server?</li>
                        <li><strong>Service mapping:</strong> Which business services depend on this CI?</li>
                        <li><strong>Business criticality:</strong> Is this a Tier 1 production system or a dev sandbox?</li>
                        <li><strong>Change history:</strong> Were there recent changes that could be related?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Developer Tip</div>
                        <p>
                            The quality of your CMDB directly impacts the value of SecOps. If your CMDB has 
                            incomplete or outdated CI records, SecOps can't properly prioritize incidents or 
                            route remediation tasks. Invest in CMDB hygiene before rolling out SecOps.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

